# Project conventions

Backend in `backend/` (Node LTS, TypeScript strict, Express 5, MongoDB, Mongoose). Frontend lives in `frontend/` and follows `frontend.mdc`.

## Architecture
- Flow: routes -> validate middleware -> controller -> service -> model.
- Controllers handle HTTP only and never import Mongoose. Services never touch req/res.
- Layout under `backend/src/`: config, models, validators, controllers, services, routes, middlewares, utils, constants. `app.ts` builds the app, `server.ts` listens.
- Routes mount at root (no `/api` prefix). Register `/sessions/available` before `/sessions/:id/*`.
- Available sessions and a user's sessions use aggregation pipelines.
- Booking and completing use one atomic `findOneAndUpdate` filtered on current status, then a follow-up lookup to pick 404 vs 409.
- No generic repository layer, no DI container.

## Errors and responses
- Success: `{ success: true, data, message? }`. Failure: `{ success: false, error: { code, message, details? } }`.
- Throw `ApiError` from services. One error middleware maps ZodError (400), CastError/bad ObjectId (400), Mongo 11000 (409), unknown (500, no stack in production).
- Validate ObjectId params with zod before touching the DB. Env is parsed with zod and fails fast.

## Code style
- async/await only. No callbacks, no `.then` chains, no `console.log` (use morgan and `utils/logger`).
- No AI-looking code: no banner comments, no JSDoc on every function, no "Step 1" narration, no emojis.
- Comment only the why, and only when it is not obvious. Short human names, small functions.
- No dead code or unused dependencies. Run `npm run typecheck` and `npm run lint` in `backend/` before finishing.
